            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Innovations in Opera</strong><br>\nMozart transformed the opera genre, creating works that are still central to the repertoire today. His collaborations with librettist Lorenzo Da Ponte resulted in the masterpieces \"The Marriage of Figaro,\" \"Don Giovanni,\" and \"Così fan tutte,\" which blended beautiful music with complex characters and plots. Mozart's operas were notable for their depth of emotion and the way they integrated music with dramatic storytelling.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Chamber Music and Piano Sonatas</strong><br>\nMozart made significant contributions to chamber music, producing works such as string quartets and quintets that displayed intricate interplay between instruments. His piano sonatas brought a new level of expressiveness to the form and were important in elevating the piano as a solo instrument. The sonatas combined lyrical melodies with challenging technical passages that showed off the performer's skill.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Final Years and Requiem</strong><br>\nThe last years of Mozart's life were marked by financial hardship, illness, and intense creativity. One of his final pieces, the \"Requiem Mass in D minor,\" was left unfinished at his death in 1791. The composition was shrouded in mystery, commissioned anonymously through an intermediary. The requiem's powerful and dramatic nature has contributed to its lasting place in the choral repertoire.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
