          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Relationship with Peers and Patrons</strong><br>\nMozart's relationship with other composers and his patrons varied throughout his life. While respected for his talents, his sometimes arrogant attitude and refusal to be subservient created tensions. However, he also enjoyed long-standing friendships and musical partnerships with figures such as Joseph Haydn, who greatly admired Mozart's work.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Mastery of Various Musical Forms</strong><br>\nMozart's mastery extended across multiple musical forms, including symphonies, concertos, chamber works, and operas. His versatility was unmatched, allowing him to compose with equal proficiency for solo instruments, small ensembles, and full orchestras. His ability to write memorable melodies and his sophisticated use of harmony and form made his music universally appealing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Influence</strong><br>\nMozart left a lasting legacy on the world of music, influencing generations of composers after him. His work embodies the balance between the structure of the Classical style and the expressive freedom that would come to define the Romantic era. Despite his early death at 35, Mozart's vast output of more than 600 compositions has secured his place as one of the greatest composers in the history of Western music.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
